Query: In the context of MVC, explain how the model and view works when model attributes are added

Response A: 

In MVC (Model-View-Controller) architecture, the model represents the data and the view represents the user interface. When model attributes are added, the view is notified and it updates itself to reflect the changes. This is done through a mechanism called "observation" or "data binding".

Here's a simple example to illustrate this:

Let's say we have a model class called `Person` with an attribute `name`. We also have a view class called `PersonView` that displays the name of the person.

```cpp
class Person {
public:
    std::string name;

    void setName(const std::string& name) {
        this->name = name;
        notifyObservers();
    }

private:
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<IObserver>> observers;

    void notifyObservers() {
        for (auto observer : observers) {
            observer->update();
        }
    }
};

class PersonView : public IObserver {
public:
    PersonView(Person* model) {
        model->addObserver(this);
    }

    void update() override {
        std::cout << "Name: " << model->name << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Person* person = new Person();
    person->name = "John";

    PersonView* view = new PersonView(person);

    person->setName("Jane");

    delete person;
    delete view;

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `Person` class has a `name` attribute and a `notifyObservers()` method. The `PersonView` class is an observer of the `Person` class and it implements the `IObserver` interface. When the `name` attribute is changed, the `notifyObservers()` method is called, which notifies all the observers (in this case, only the `PersonView` object). The `PersonView` object then updates its display to reflect the change.

This is just a simple example, and in real-world applications, the observation mechanism may be more complex and involve more classes and components. However, the basic principle remains the same: when model attributes are added, the view is notified and it updates itself to reflect the changes.

